\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces The geometry of poisoning, in stages (\cref  {ex:stages}). (a) The shadow of the fibre on two never-observed quantities. From a rigid CH-facet point (star), route A (diamonds) stays a point; route B opens into polygons as poisoned units are added (labels), with the maximum-entropy law inside. (b) The identified interval: the same share flips some rule on both routes, but flipping every rule off the facet takes nearly ten times as many units. (c) Distance to the contextuality boundary. (d) A PR-box overreach: from the rigid point any contextual push empties the fibre; from inside, exactly where the facet slacks predict. (e) Certified radii against actual cheapest flips. (f) Width change against edit cost: the dimension jumps, the width obeys the Lipschitz bound.}}{70}{figure.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:deform}{{24}{70}{The geometry of poisoning, in stages (\cref {ex:stages}). (a) The shadow of the fibre on two never-observed quantities. From a rigid CH-facet point (star), route A (diamonds) stays a point; route B opens into polygons as poisoned units are added (labels), with the maximum-entropy law inside. (b) The identified interval: the same share flips some rule on both routes, but flipping every rule off the facet takes nearly ten times as many units. (c) Distance to the contextuality boundary. (d) A PR-box overreach: from the rigid point any contextual push empties the fibre; from inside, exactly where the facet slacks predict. (e) Certified radii against actual cheapest flips. (f) Width change against edit cost: the dimension jumps, the width obeys the Lipschitz bound}{figure.24}{}}
\newlabel{fig:deform@cref}{{[figure][24][]24}{[1][70][]70}}
